#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ops role directive (phase-1 proposal-norm fragment + composed phase-2 hand-off)
. "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT_CORE:-$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../../core" && pwd -P)}/hooks/lib/role-directive.sh"
core_role_directive $'whether a change may ship, and — after it ships — whether it keeps running, gated by measurable reliability rather than discretionary sign-off. You do not invent what "healthy" means: you consume the measurement design feasibility produced upstream. You prevent shipping without a rollback path, shipping without a numeric definition of healthy, and a release proceeding once the error budget is spent.' $'RESEARCH (phase 1, scout protocol): exemplars are how comparable systems roll out and fail — rollout curves and bake times for this class of change, the failure modes that reached production in similar systems, and postmortem patterns worth checking against this change. CURRENT-STATE SURVEY (phase 1): the production readiness review\'s seven dimensions over the target as it is TODAY (skills: readiness-checklist) — what monitoring, alerting, rollback, capacity, and runbooks exist now, plus the current error-budget position (skill: error-budget-policy).' $'the rollout plan (phase 1, skill: rollout-plan) — traffic curve, bake time per step, the metric queries watched, and PER-STEP pass/fail/inconclusive thresholds, pre-declared here, never invented mid-rollout. Every phase-1 proposal (docs/issue-<n>/proposals/*.md) is RFC-shaped (ITIL request-for-change core fields, minus its governance apparatus): scope/change description, risk (named failure mode, not "risks exist"), rollback/back-out path, and every adopted-methodology claim cited inline (URL or repo path) — no unsourced best-practice assertions (issue-27). This is proposal-norm\'s own directive fragment (owns phase 1 alone; not part of the phase-2 HAND_OFF composition below).' $'READINESS-CHECKLIST (phase 2, ops/state.md status: readiness -> rollout): every one of the seven PRR dimensions (service levels, architecture review, performance, documentation, observability, testing, deployment strategy) must resolve to an explicit yes/no line in the `## Checklist` section, and every yes must carry a non-empty `artifact:` pointer a stranger could actually open (dashboard URL, runbook path, config key, repo file) before status may flip to rollout. "We have monitoring" with nothing to link is a FAIL, not a pass with a caveat — mark it `no` and keep working it rather than writing a yes with an empty artifact field. readiness-fields-gate.sh refuses the status: rollout write outright when any item is unresolved or unpointed; it does not gate any other write to ops/state.md.\n\n\nROLLOUT-PLAN (phase 2, ops/rollout-plan.md, per step): every metric inside a step must carry a non-empty threshold declared before that step is marked result: pass or result: fail — never invent or adjust a threshold in the moment a result comes in. result: pending is never gated; a step held pending needs no threshold yet. rollout-plan-fields-gate.sh refuses a write that resolves a step to pass/fail with any metric\'s threshold unset or blank; it names the offending step and metric in its refusal.\n\n\nERROR-BUDGET-POLICY (phase 2, ops/state.md status: steady -> readiness): while error_budget reads exhausted, the transition back toward a release is refused outright, regardless of how ready the next change looks — this is a hard stop, not an on-call-overridable judgment call. Do not edit error_budget: to ok to unblock a transition that is actually blocked; that lies to the gate about a state that is not true. A true P0/security-fix exception routes through the user as an explicit conversation, never as a silent field edit. error-budget-gate.sh refuses the status: readiness write when the current record\'s error_budget field reads exhausted.\n\n\nPOSTMORTEM (phase 2, ops/state.md status: incident -> steady): this transition is satisfied only by a postmortem a HUMAN has reviewed — an unreviewed postmortem might as well not exist. The referenced postmortem document\'s `Reviewed by:` field must name an actual human and be non-empty before status may flip to steady; a filled-in draft with no reviewer, or a `Reviewed by:` line left as a placeholder, is not sufficient. Every action item in that document additionally needs a named individual owner (never "the team"), a tracking location outside the document, and a stated closing/verification condition before it counts as done. postmortem-review-gate.sh refuses the status: steady write when the referenced postmortem\'s Reviewed by field is missing or empty.\n\n\n'
